# Two panels for the d26_s1 result: the wild-type Pikp-1 HMA / AVR-PikF complex
# and the negative-steered d26_s1 prediction, drawn in the same orientation at
# one camera so the rebuilt design region 1 can be compared directly.
#
# The previous version of this figure was two screen captures taken at
# different scales, which is why its caption had to admit the panels were not
# size-matched. Here both models are superimposed on the fixed N-terminal block
# that neither design touches, and the camera is set once and never moved
# between the two saves, so the scale is identical by construction.
#
# Run from this directory:
#   ChimeraX --offscreen --nogui --exit --script render_wt_vs_design5.cxc

open data/wt_pikp1_avrpikf.pdb
open data/d26_s1_steered_design01_s0.pdb
rename #1 wt
rename #2 d26

# Fix the window aspect first, otherwise the saved image inherits whatever
# shape the ChimeraX window happens to have and the panels need rescaling
# afterwards, which is the problem this figure is being rebuilt to avoid.
windowsize 2000 1500

dssp
hide atoms
show cartoon
set bgColor white
# Flat shading and silhouettes, matching render_design_regions.cxc so the two
# structure figures in this chapter look like one set.
lighting flat
graphics silhouettes true width 1.5

# Shared palette with the RFdiffusion design-region figure.
color cornflower blue
color /B light green
# Design region 1: 33-45 in the wild type, 33-51 in d26_s1, which samples 19
# residues against the wild-type 13. Design region 2 is the C-terminal six.
color #1/A:33-45 red
color #1/A:73-78 purple
color #2/A:33-51 red
color #2/A:79-84 purple
# The three negative-steering mutations this representative carries. They sit
# on the competing AVR-Pia surface, outside both design regions.
color #2/A:22,26,31 goldenrod

# Superimpose on the EFFECTOR, not on the receptor scaffold. Both complexes
# hold the same AVR-PikF, so matching on it puts the effector in the same place
# in both panels and any difference the reader sees is a difference in the
# receptor. Aligning on the receptor N-terminal block instead left the two
# effectors in different positions, which is what the figure is comparing.
align #2/B@CA toAtoms #1/B@CA

# Same camera as panel A of the RFdiffusion design-region figure, so the
# interface is presented from the orientation that figure established. Framed
# on the union of the two models so neither is clipped, then left alone.
view orient
turn x 225
turn y 345
turn z 45
view
zoom 1.15

hide #2 models
# 4800 px so that after cropping to the ink the panel still holds more pixels
# than it is printed with. At 3000 the crop came out 881 px and was being
# upsampled to 1260 px on the page, which is what produced the ragged black
# edges along the silhouettes.
save renders/panel_a_wt.png width 4800 supersample 3 transparentBackground true
show #2 models
hide #1 models
save renders/panel_b_d26_s1.png width 4800 supersample 3 transparentBackground true

# The contact the extended loop could make. The closest Ca-Ca pair between
# design region 1 and the effector is Thr41 to Gln34 at 6.60 A, but the side
# chains are more specific: Asp42 of the rebuilt loop puts its carboxylate
# 3.72 A from the Arg8 guanidinium of AVR-PikF, inside the usual 4 A
# salt-bridge criterion. That is the interaction worth drawing.
# The atoms must be displayed or the distance pseudobond has nothing to hang
# from and silently does not draw.
show #2/A:42 #2/B:8 atoms
style #2/A:42 #2/B:8 stick
size #2/A:42 #2/B:8 stickRadius 0.15
color #2/A:42 & sidechain byhetero
color #2/B:8 & sidechain byhetero
distance #2/A:42@OD1 #2/B:8@NH1
distance style color black dashes 8 radius 0.09 decimalPlaces 2
label #2/A:42 text "Asp42" height 1.7 color black offset 0.9,0.9,0
label #2/B:8 text "Arg8" height 1.7 color black offset 0.9,-1.5,0

# Third panel: an actual close-up. The previous version framed the residues
# but left the whole complex displayed, so the frame filled with the rest of
# the structure and the panel looked like the overview again. Here everything
# outside the contact neighbourhood is hidden first, which is what gives the
# labels clear space to sit in.
hide #2 cartoon
show #2/A:38-46 cartoon
show #2/B:5-12 cartoon
# Ser39 also reaches the effector, at 3.68 A to Asn11, so it is shown too.
# It is a hydroxyl to a backbone-adjacent carbon rather than a charged pair,
# so it is drawn but not measured.
show #2/A:39 #2/B:11 atoms
style #2/A:39 #2/B:11 stick
size #2/A:39 #2/B:11 stickRadius 0.12
color #2/A:39 & sidechain byhetero
color #2/B:11 & sidechain byhetero
# Frame the shown neighbourhood, then ease out just enough for the labels.
# zoom takes a factor, so values below 1 zoom OUT; 0.42 shrank the close-up to
# a fifth of the frame.
view #2/A:38-46 #2/B:5-12
# Spun about the vertical screen axis, like turning a plate on a table, so the
# receptor loop and the effector separate left to right and the gap between
# them becomes a vertical band of whitespace for the labels. turn z instead
# rotates the whole scene in the plane of the page, like a clock on a wall,
# which moves the picture without opening any space up.
turn y 40
zoom 0.80

# Labels placed well off the atoms, into the empty part of the frame, and
# larger now that the panel is a close-up rather than a whole domain.
# The distance monitor draws its own value onto the dashed line. Deleting all
# labels here removes it, so the residue names and one value label can then be
# placed where they do not sit on anything. Doing this the other way round left
# two "3.72" labels at different sizes and dropped the Asp42 name, because an
# atom label on OD1 displaces the residue label on 42.
distance style color black dashes 8 radius 0.05
label delete

label #2/A:42 text "Asp42" height 1.1 color black offset -4.8,-1.8,0
label #2/A:39 text "Ser39" height 1.1 color black offset -4.8,2.4,0
label #2/B:8 text "Arg8" height 1.1 color black offset 3.6,1.6,0
label #2/A:42@OD1 text "3.72 A" height 1.0 color black offset 0.4,-3.6,0
distance style color black dashes 8 radius 0.05 decimalPlaces 2

save renders/panel_d_contact.png width 4800 supersample 3 transparentBackground true

# Restore the full cartoon so anything rendered after this is unaffected.
show #2 cartoon

# Both together, in case an overlay reads better than a side-by-side.
show #1 models
color #1 grey
transparency #1 55
save renders/panel_c_overlay.png width 4800 supersample 3 transparentBackground true
